{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Extend Pass 1 by introducing a total, stable preference order over arguments and filtering the attack relation so that an attack is retained only if the attacker is not strictly less preferred than the target. The grounded extension is then computed on the filtered graph.","counterconditions":["If the preference order is not total or is not stable across runs, the filtered graph may vary, breaking determinism of the fixed point.","If the preference computation is not polynomial in the size of the argument graph, the overall cost bound is violated.","If a committed root is attacked by an argument of equal preference, the root may be defeated unless equal-preference attacks are also removed or the root is assigned a strictly maximal preference."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0909868ae3d22218","quote":"any attacker of validity_node attacks the warrant"},{"block":"0c302d11d621778","quote":"The grounded extension itself was never the problem: given the edges, the label computation is a unique deterministic fixed point."}],"mechanism":"1. Define a total preorder over arguments (e.g., by provenance, warrant strength, or a fixed priority list). 2. For each attack edge (a,b) in the original graph, keep it iff preference(a) >= preference(b). 3. Assign maximal preference to all committed roots, so they cannot be attacked by any lower-preference argument. 4. Run the standard Dung grounded extension algorithm on the filtered graph. The result is unique and skeptical because the filtered graph is still a finite directed graph and the grounded extension is unique.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7}],"simulation_proposals":[]}